/* Wealth Lab · Module 8 Academic: Stock Markets & Company Analysis · 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM8Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m8-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","lessonIds":["L8.S1","L8.S2","L8.S3","L8.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L8.S1","title":"What a Stock Actually Owns","subtitle":"Security rights, dilution, financing, corporate governance and evidence from issuer filings","prerequisites":["L1.S1 assets and liabilities","L4.S1 ownership versus debt claims","L7.S3 present value and risk discounting"],"objectives":["Trace a common equity claim through corporate legal and financial structure","Calculate equity capitalization, enterprise value and share ownership","Model primary issuance, dilution, options and buybacks","Distinguish voting/control rights, fiduciary responsibilities and economic exposure","Read annual-report business, capital-stock and risk sections","Assess why leverage and concentration increase shareholder downside"],"sections":[{"title":"A share is a residual claim, not a piece of an asset you can withdraw","body":["Common stock represents a financial and legal interest in the corporation. A shareholder's exact rights depend on governing documents, state law and the class of shares. The corporation owns its cash, factories, contracts and intellectual property; a person buying one share generally cannot walk into the company and withdraw a proportional quantity of its machinery or bank deposits. Common shareholders bear residual economic exposure after creditors and higher-priority security claims and may hold specified voting rights. A board's capital-allocation decision can substantially affect a small shareholder's wealth without creating a direct right to demand a dividend.","The difference between primary issuance and secondary trading is fundamental. In a primary sale of newly issued common shares, the issuer can receive net funds after underwriting and other issuance costs; the new share count can dilute existing holders. In an ordinary secondary trade, the buyer pays another investor rather than the company's treasury. Secondary markets provide liquidity and price discovery, but buying stock on an exchange does not normally provide fresh working capital directly to the business.","Suppose a fictional issuer has 10 million common shares outstanding at $40. Its quoted equity market capitalization is $400 million, but that amount is not its actual cash balance or its liquidation payout. A person buying 25 shares holds 25/10,000,000=0.00025% of the outstanding common class before other rights. If the company's debts overwhelm its realizable assets, common shareholders may recover nothing despite a prior $400 million quoted equity market value.","Due diligence starts with the Form 10-K business description, share-class and capitalization footnotes, legal proceedings, risk factors, governance disclosures and relevant corporate actions. Treat insider voting control and common economic percentage separately. A widely advertised app ticker is not proof that its security is ordinary voting stock or that the issuer has no preferred or convertible claims. Assess the actual claim, not a recognizable brand name."],"worked":["10 million common shares × $40 per share = $400 million equity market capitalization.","25 shares / 10,000,000 shares = 0.0000025 of shares = 0.00025% ownership of that class.","Another investor purchases 25 shares at $40: $1,000 before transaction fees goes to selling investors in an ordinary exchange trade.","A later new-share issuance can change the denominator and voting percentage.","Share price and market cap do not reveal cash available to distribute or residual liquidation proceeds."],"practice":[["Why does buying $1,000 of an existing listed corporation's shares not automatically fund a new corporate factory?","In the usual secondary-market transaction, cash goes to the selling shareholder. New issuance is a different capital-raising process that sends net proceeds to the issuer."],["Why is a $400 million equity capitalization not equivalent to $400 million in the company's checking account?","Market capitalization reflects price times outstanding common shares. Cash, debts, property and other balance-sheet components must be independently identified in the filings."]]},{"title":"Share count, dilution and fully diluted economic interests","body":["Ownership percentage equals shares held divided by the applicable total count, but different counts answer different questions. Shares authorized, issued, treasury-held, outstanding, basic weighted-average and diluted weighted-average shares are not interchangeable. A company's balance sheet date outstanding shares may differ from the income statement's period-average shares. Diluted EPS generally reflects relevant potentially dilutive securities under financial reporting requirements; it does not mean every option, warrant or restricted stock unit has already been exercised.","Suppose a company initially has 10 million shares outstanding and issues 2 million new shares at $20, ignoring fees. It receives $40 million cash. A holder owning 100,000 shares initially held 1%; after issuance the same 100,000/12,000,000=0.8333%. The holder's ownership fraction fell, but economic shareholder value might still improve if the new funding generates a return above its cost. Dilution percentage alone is not proof that management destroyed wealth.","Stock-based compensation grants may increase future shares and impose economic cost even when the company does not pay the employee cash at the award date. Buybacks can offset dilution, reduce shares outstanding or change equity financing, but can also use cash needed for business investment. Analysts should inspect the statement of stockholders' equity, cash flow financing section, footnotes, option exercise terms and share repurchase disclosures for a complete share-count bridge.","A serious valuation model projects shares as well as firm value. If a forecast business has $600 million of equity value after a financing and 12 million shares, price-equivalent valuation is $50 per share. Dividing by the stale 10 million starting share count yields a misleading $60. A company can grow profit and still deliver little per-share benefit if the share base grows quickly."],"worked":["Initial 10,000,000 shares; 100,000 shareholder shares ⇒ 1.00% of outstanding class.","2,000,000 new shares issued at $20 ⇒ issuer receives $40,000,000 gross proceeds.","New outstanding 12,000,000; holder unchanged 100,000/12,000,000≈0.8333%.","Equity value $600 million / 12 million shares = $50 per share, not $60 based on stale shares.","A decline in percentage ownership can coexist with increased total corporate value, depending on financing use."],"practice":[["Why should EPS forecasting consider stock options and restricted-share awards?","Potential dilution and compensation costs can affect future share counts and cash/economic claims; using unchanged shares may inflate per-share value."],["Does 16.7% relative dilution in this example prove the financing was destructive?","No. Capital raised may finance positive-value operations. Analysts must compare proceeds, project returns, the resulting capital structure and future value per share."]]},{"title":"Equity capitalization, enterprise value and priority of creditors","body":["Market capitalization reflects the market value of common equity. Enterprise value is a capital-structure-neutral approximation of operating business value, frequently computed as market capitalization plus interest-bearing debt, preferred claims and noncontrolling interests minus excess cash, subject to leases and other adjustments. It should not be confused with the price for one class of publicly traded stock. Companies with identical $400 million common market caps may have sharply different debt burdens and assets available to equity.","In a simplified company with $400 million equity market cap, $150 million interest-bearing debt and $50 million excess cash, enterprise value is $500 million. If another company has no debt and $50 million cash, its comparable simplified EV is $350 million. Their common stock prices cannot be judged comparable from market capitalization alone. Debt can amplify earnings during strong operations and make common equity fragile during recessions or refinancing stress.","Creditor priority is not the same as guaranteed full repayment in distress; liens, secured status, statutory claims and bankruptcy rules determine recoveries. For a hypothetical liquidation realizing $200 million from assets when secured and unsecured claims total $230 million with higher priority, common stock may have zero residual value. Book shareholders' equity on historical-cost accounting is not necessarily the liquidation realizable value of the company's property.","Enterprise-value ratios must match the correct operating flow. EV/EBITDA compares enterprise value with an operating earnings proxy before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization, but EBITDA ignores capital expenditure and working-capital cash needs and is not free cash flow. Equity price/earnings uses equity market capitalization and earnings available to common shareholders. Mixed numerator and denominator definitions produce seductive but meaningless comparisons."],"worked":["Equity cap $400m + debt $150m − excess cash $50m ⇒ simplified EV $500m.","Alternative with same equity cap, no debt and $50m excess cash ⇒ EV $350m.","At EV $500m and illustrative EBITDA $50m, EV/EBITDA=10×.","EV/EBITDA does not equal equity P/E or prove the company can distribute $50m in cash.","Liquidation asset proceeds $200m against higher-priority $230m claims can leave common residual $0."],"practice":[["Why can two companies with identical equity market caps have substantially different enterprise values?","Debt, cash, preferred equity, minority claims and other financing adjustments differ; common share value is only one component of the operating enterprise's funding."],["Why is dividing market capitalization by EBITDA an unreliable substitute for EV/EBITDA?","The numerator values equity alone while EBITDA is an enterprise-level operating measure available before financing costs, making the ratio internally inconsistent."]]},{"title":"Voting rights, board oversight and governance incentives","body":["Corporate governance defines who can make decisions over operations, capital, acquisitions, dividends and executive compensation. Most common stock structures confer some voting rights, but dual-class arrangements can give founders disproportionate control relative to economic ownership. A shareholder owning a small noncontrolling position usually cannot independently replace management, liquidate assets or compel a dividend. Proxy statements and issuer governance filings can describe board elections, compensation packages and related-party transactions.","Different share classes can have the same market-price risk but different control rights. A two-class firm may issue Class A shares with one vote each and Class B with ten votes each, producing a voting structure far different from share ownership. If founders hold 2 million ten-vote shares while public investors hold 8 million one-vote shares, founders control 20/28≈71.43% of votes despite 20% of shares. Check whether conversion rights, shareholder agreements or legal constraints modify those simplified figures.","Governance analysis is not a popularity contest about executive personalities. Identify whether compensation rewards revenue or profitable free cash flow, whether related-party deals transfer value away from ordinary investors, and whether dilution or capital allocation benefits controlling holders differently. Shareholders are exposed to agency costs when those managing resources make decisions not aligned with long-term value creation.","Research materials include the annual meeting proxy statement, Form 10-K governance references, charter, financial footnotes and timely Form 8-K disclosures for material events. A decision memo must identify actual controlling votes and disclosure sources rather than infer control solely from founder titles or the number of social-media followers."],"worked":["Public Class A: 8m shares ×1 vote = 8m votes.","Founders Class B: 2m shares ×10 votes = 20m votes.","Total votes 28m; founder voting control 20/28≈71.43% despite 2/10=20% economic shares.","Different control and cash-flow rights may affect minority-investor protections.","A common share count is insufficient to infer voting authority in a dual-class issuer."],"practice":[["How can a founder control board elections while owning less than a majority of the economic shares?","Dual-class voting rights can assign more votes per share to the founder's class, making voting control differ from financial ownership."],["Where should investors examine governance conflicts and executive pay arrangements?","Proxy filings, company charters, related-party and compensation disclosures, board information and relevant SEC documents are primary sources."]]},{"title":"Business model economics, competitive advantages and cash conversion","body":["Stock ownership should be analyzed through the economics of the underlying business. A company creates sales from products or services, incurs costs, reinvests in assets and requires financing. Recurring customer demand is not identical to attractive unit economics: acquiring and servicing one customer might cost more than the lifetime gross profit that customer provides. Competitive advantages such as switching costs, networks or specialized logistics require evidence in margins, retention and capital productivity.","Model revenue as price times units, then separate volume, pricing, cost per unit and recurring operating expenses. A fictional company selling 100,000 units at $50 generates $5 million revenue. If direct cost is $30/unit, gross profit is $2 million or 40% gross margin. If fixed operating expenses are $1.5 million, operating profit is only $500,000 or 10% operating margin. A 10% decline in sales volume with unchanged cost structure reduces operating profit to $300,000, a 40% drop, illustrating operating leverage.","Reported revenue growth can be financed by aggressive credit terms that leave receivables uncollected. Gross margin can look attractive while warranty liability, returns, stock compensation or customer concentration threaten cash. A company dependent on one client for 40% of revenue is exposed to a risk that an industry index cannot eliminate. Read segment notes and concentration disclosures to understand the issuer's actual exposures.","A research memo should articulate the connection between product advantage, cash returns and equity valuation. Avoid equating a popular product with shareholder returns, particularly if an attractive business trades at a price implying unrealistic growth. The investor must quantify unit economics and then ask which expectations are already reflected in the market price."],"worked":["Units sold 100,000 × price $50 = $5m revenue.","Direct unit cost $30 ×100,000=$3m, gross profit $2m, gross margin 40%.","Fixed operating costs $1.5m ⇒ operating profit $0.5m and margin 10%.","Units decline 10% to 90,000 ⇒ gross profit $1.8m; fixed operating cost $1.5m ⇒ EBIT $0.3m.","Operating income drops 40% despite revenue dropping 10%, revealing operating leverage."],"practice":[["Why may a company's 20% revenue increase fail to create comparable shareholder value?","Selling costs, additional capital and dilution, weak collections, margins and price paid may offset growing revenue; revenue is not distributable cash."],["What does operating leverage mean in the unit-economics example?","Fixed costs cause operating profit to react more strongly to volume shifts than revenue does, so a moderate sales decline can sharply reduce profit."]]},{"title":"Industry concentration, competition and nondiversifiable risk","body":["Company-specific risk includes product obsolescence, management failure, customer dependence, accounting problems and bankruptcy. Industry risk includes regulation, commodity input shocks, technological shifts and shared demand cycles. Broad market risk includes recessions and changes in discount rates affecting many issuers simultaneously. Owning stock in several firms within one sector may diversify individual mishaps while preserving heavy exposure to the same demand driver.","A portfolio with 50% of its value in one issuer can lose 25% from the portfolio if that issuer declines 50% and other assets do not move. No plausible stock-valuation thesis should ignore this concentration arithmetic. A business owner whose job depends on a specific industry may also concentrate personal income and investments in the same cycle. A Connecticut household that needs a house down payment within a year should not use a single-stock price target as if it were cash.","Competitive analysis should identify the addressable market, customer acquisition cost, competitors, barriers to entry, supplier power, pricing history and cash reinvestment needs. A patent can expire; network effects can reverse; high current margins can attract new rivals. Forecast errors are particularly costly when stock prices assume years of exceptional growth or near-perfect profit margins.","An investment risk register names the factor, evidence, probability range if defensible, exposure, mitigating action and invalidation condition. It must also clarify the downside under insolvency: limited liability means an ordinary fully paid common shareholder generally risks the investment amount, but leverage through margin borrowing or derivatives can create additional obligations. Do not convert this general principle into legal advice about a specific investment account."],"worked":["Portfolio $20,000; 50% in one issuer = $10,000 concentrated exposure.","Single issuer declines 50% ⇒ issuer value $5,000, portfolio $15,000 if other holdings unchanged.","Portfolio loss 25% from that one stock position.","An emergency down-payment bucket should not be funded by a hoped-for concentrated-stock rebound.","Diversification reduces many issuer-specific risks but cannot guarantee against marketwide losses."],"practice":[["Does owning ten technology stocks guarantee broad diversification against technology sector risk?","No. Shared industry valuation, regulation, business cycles and investor demand can correlate losses across issuers despite multiple tickers."],["How should an investment policy respond to a large single-stock exposure when the owner also works for the issuer?","Assess combined job-income and portfolio concentration, set position/risk limits and protect near-term cash needs; the employer exposure may compound a single economic shock."]]},{"title":"The primary-source company ownership memorandum","body":["An academically defensible company memo begins with exact security identification: issuer, trading symbol, legal security class, exchange, filing date, recent capitalization and share count. Retrieve the Form 10-K or, for relevant events, Form 10-Q and Form 8-K from EDGAR. Explain how the company earns money and which businesses or geographic regions drive revenue. Use the notes to securities outstanding to reconcile potentially dilutive claims and the proxy statement to identify control.","Present the capital structure on one consistent date: common equity market capitalization, debt, relevant preferred claims, cash, adjusted enterprise value, lease obligations and debt maturities. A small market capitalization may coexist with substantial debt. Inspect restricted cash and determine whether subtracting all stated cash from EV overstates funds distributable to buyers or shareholders. Avoid inventing exact stock-price or filing facts unless the documents provide them.","Write three investment theses: an upside case, a skeptical base case and a downside case. Each needs revenue, margins, reinvestment and dilution assumptions. Define what would prove the thesis wrong, for instance recurring negative free cash flow, widening customer churn or refinancing beyond cash-generation capacity. A thoughtful memo can conclude 'no investment' when financial statements are unclear or the household's goal is too near.","Close with source citations, dates, uncertainty and account suitability. Record the price you would hypothetically pay, valuation ranges, identified counterevidence, concentration and how an unexpected earnings miss affects the portfolio. The goal is to demonstrate analysis skill, not recommend a brand or predict price movement from a single news headline."],"worked":["Document a fictional company's 10m outstanding shares and $40 illustrative price, creating $400m equity market cap.","Add $150m interest-bearing debt and subtract $50m truly excess cash ⇒ simplified EV $500m.","List whether the same firm has 2m potential new shares and how that changes forecast per-share value.","Include historical sales and free cash flow from issuer filings rather than price charts alone.","Require at least one clearly falsifiable counter-thesis and an explicit no-buy condition."],"practice":[["What makes a corporate analysis memorandum auditable by a second investor?","It names the exact issuer, security class and filing versions, reconciles every financial measure to reported periods and documents assumptions and counterevidence."],["Why can an analytically attractive equity be unsuitable for a person purchasing a home in six months?","The household may lack capacity to withstand an interim stock drawdown before closing, even if the company could be attractive for long-term risk capital."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC investor guide to 10-K","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/researching-investments/how-read-10-k"],["SEC EDGAR company filing search","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"],["SEC Beginner's Guide to Financial Statements","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/begfinstmtguide.htm"],["SEC Annual Reports and Proxy Statements","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/annual-report"],["FINRA Investor Insights","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/stocks"]],"assessment":{"title":"Corporate ownership and dilution forensic memo","prompt":"Investigate a hypothetical 10-million-share corporation priced at $40 that issues two million new shares at $20, carries $150 million of debt and $50 million excess cash, and has dual-class votes. Compute original and new capitalization, 100,000-share ownership percentages, enterprise value, and value per diluted share under $600 million equity assumption. Analyze operating leverage for unit sales and create a source-dated Form 10-K research plan including governance, issuer risks and a downside portfolio allocation.","rubric":["Security identification and legal claims (20%)","Market capitalization, EV and dilution math (25%)","Governance/control rights and capital structure (20%)","Business model, operating leverage and concentration (20%)","Primary-source research and investment memo quality (15%)"]}},{"id":"L8.S2","title":"Reading the Three Statements","subtitle":"Double-entry accounting, accrual earnings, working capital, cash-flow bridges and earnings quality","prerequisites":["L1.S1 the wealth equation","L8.S1 corporate equity and liabilities","L7.S3 discounted cash flows"],"objectives":["Reconcile assets=liabilities+equity across corporate transactions","Interpret revenue, margins, expenses and earnings by period","Bridge net income to operating cash flow with working capital","Reconstruct free cash flow and owner-available cash after financing","Compute liquidity, leverage, turnover and return-on-capital ratios","Recognize accrual estimates and red flags in public-company filings"],"sections":[{"title":"Accounting equation, statement periods and financial reporting controls","body":["The balance sheet is a dated snapshot of assets, liabilities and equity: A=L+E. The income statement records recognized revenue and expenses across a period; the cash flow statement classifies cash generated or used by operating, investing and financing activities across that period. The statement of shareholders' equity explains changes in retained earnings, accumulated comprehensive income, paid-in equity, distributions and treasury stock. All four statements are linked by underlying transactions even though each answers a different question.","Double-entry logic preserves the accounting equation. A company borrows $100,000 in cash: cash assets rise $100,000 and debt liabilities rise $100,000, but equity does not jump because the loan is not profit. A cash purchase of a $40,000 machine transfers cash into property and equipment without immediately creating an expense equal to purchase price. Later depreciation expense spreads a qualifying cost across useful life under applicable accounting assumptions. Cash and accounting income therefore can move in different periods.","Read the financial statement period and the audit context. A fiscal year may not match the calendar year, a quarter is not a full-year period, and comparable figures can be restated. US public companies generally prepare financial statements under US GAAP, while other companies may report under IFRS or distinct standards; models must be normalized before cross-company comparison. Consolidation can include controlled subsidiaries and noncontrolling ownership interests, which complicate simple parent-shareholder ratios.","Use the SEC's financial-statement guide and the 10-K financial statements and footnotes as primary sources. Examine auditor opinions and material weaknesses in internal controls, accounting changes and estimates. An attractive reported net income number cannot independently establish cash collections, contractual debt capacity or reliability of financial controls."],"worked":["Beginning assets $500,000, liabilities $300,000, equity $200,000: accounting equation balances.","Borrow $100,000 cash ⇒ assets $600,000 and liabilities $400,000, equity $200,000.","Buy $40,000 equipment with cash ⇒ asset mix changes, total assets remain $600,000 initially.","Subsequent depreciation can reduce reported income and property book value without an equal current cash outflow.","Financial statement dates and accounting bases must align across comparisons."],"practice":[["Why does a corporation's $100,000 new bank loan not constitute $100,000 of net earnings?","Cash increases with a matching repayment liability; financing creates neither revenue nor residual equity by itself under the assumed transaction."],["What is the missing fourth statement when students discuss only the balance sheet, income statement and cash flow statement?","The statement of stockholders' equity explains share issuance, repurchases, dividends, retained earnings and other equity changes across the reporting period."]]},{"title":"Income statement margins and the layers of economic performance","body":["An income statement begins with recognized revenues and subtracts cost of sales to derive gross profit. It then accounts for selling, general, administrative, research and other operating expenses to estimate operating income. Financing costs, other nonoperating gains and losses and income tax lead to net income. Basic earnings per share divides earnings attributable to common shareholders by weighted-average common shares under the applicable reporting convention; diluted earnings per share accounts for specified potentially dilutive securities. Analysts must match EPS to the right numerator and denominator.","Consider a fictional company with $1 million in net sales, $600,000 cost of revenue, $250,000 operating expenses, $30,000 interest expense and $30,000 tax expense. Gross profit is $400,000 (40% margin), operating income $150,000 (15% margin), pretax income $120,000 and reported net income $90,000 (9% net margin). A 10% decline in sales does not always produce a 10% decline in profit when fixed expenses, pricing and volume costs respond differently.","Revenue recognition requires judgment. Credit sales can increase recognized revenue before customers pay. Refund rights, customer incentives, unbilled receivables, deferred contract liabilities and multi-year service obligations can affect timing. An increase in accounts receivable faster than sales can signal changing payment terms, weaker collections or simply business mix; it is a question to investigate rather than automatic evidence of fraudulent reporting. Segment margins can conceal a loss-making product behind profitable divisions.","Follow the footnotes and MD&A for accounting policies, customer concentration, restructuring items, changes in estimated useful lives and one-off gains. Companies often discuss adjusted earnings measures, which can exclude costs management considers exceptional. Reconstruct all adjustments and do not treat recurring stock compensation or recurring restructuring charges as imaginary costs. A ratio is a useful diagnostic, not proof the business has durable cash earnings."],"worked":["Revenue $1,000,000 − cost of revenue $600,000 ⇒ gross profit $400,000 and gross margin 40%.","Operating expenses $250,000 ⇒ operating income $150,000 and operating margin 15%.","Interest $30,000 and income tax $30,000 ⇒ net income $90,000 and margin 9%.","At 30,000 weighted-average basic shares, basic EPS would be $3 in this simplified case.","An unusual gain included in net income should not be assumed repeatable without evidence."],"practice":[["Why can a company display a strong gross margin but weak free cash flow?","Operating costs, interest, taxes, working capital investment and capital expenditures can consume cash after gross profit; accrual timing also differs."],["Why is dividing net income by shares outstanding on the final day potentially different from reported basic EPS?","Financial reporting generally uses weighted-average shares for the earnings period and earnings attributable to the appropriate common stock class, not necessarily the period-end share count."]]},{"title":"Operating cash flow: converting accrual profit into actual cash","body":["Operating cash flow under the indirect presentation begins with net income and adjusts for noncash charges plus changes in relevant operating assets and liabilities. Adding back depreciation does not mean depreciation was a fake economic cost; it corrects the timing of a noncash accounting expense relative to current cash outlays. Rising receivables typically use cash relative to booked revenue; rising payables can temporarily preserve cash by delaying supplier payments. Each adjustment reflects an actual balance-sheet movement and requires correct sign.","Suppose net income is $90,000, depreciation is $20,000, receivables rise $35,000, inventory rises $15,000 and payables rise $10,000, with no other changes. Simplified operating cash flow is $90,000+$20,000−$35,000−$15,000+$10,000=$70,000. Positive profit therefore exceeds operating cash collected and retained under this illustrative bridge. If receivables climb faster than reported revenue for several years, investigate customers, collectability and revenue recognition rather than assuming growth guarantees cash.","Working capital links income statement to funding requirements. Accounts receivable are customer amounts owed; inventory represents held inputs and goods; accounts payable represent amounts owed to suppliers. A business can grow revenue and require additional cash for raw materials and longer customer credit. Conversely, some high-turnover retailers receive customer cash before paying suppliers and can have favorable working-capital economics. A cash flow surge from stretching payables may not be a permanent margin improvement.","Cash flow needs context: an operating cash inflow can coexist with major debt due next year; the company may borrow to finance share repurchases or buy equipment, both of which belong outside operating cash flow. Compare multi-year cash generation with earnings, working-capital balances, and cash-flow statement footnotes. Because cash balances vary with financing and investment decisions, the existence of cash in a bank account at year end does not by itself prove sustainable operations."],"worked":["Net income $90,000 + depreciation $20,000 = $110,000 pre-working-capital bridge.","Receivables increase $35,000 ⇒ subtract $35,000; inventory rises $15,000 ⇒ subtract $15,000.","Accounts payable increase $10,000 ⇒ add $10,000 in this simplified model.","Operating cash flow $110,000−$35,000−$15,000+$10,000=$70,000.","Operating cash differs from net income by negative $20,000 despite positive reported profit."],"practice":[["Why is an increase in accounts receivable usually a subtraction when reconciling income to cash under an indirect method?","A company can record sales before collecting payment; higher unpaid customer balances mean less cash than accrual revenue would suggest."],["Why does adding back depreciation in CFO not prove capital equipment is free to replace?","Depreciation was a noncash current expense, but replacing or maintaining physical equipment can demand substantial investing cash outflows over time."]]},{"title":"Investing and financing activities: free cash flow is not cash in the bank","body":["Operating, investing and financing cash classifications distinguish business generation from financing choices. Purchases of equipment and facilities normally appear as investing outflows. Debt issuance, principal repayment, stock issuance, repurchases and dividends normally appear as financing flows under US GAAP; some classifications can differ under other accounting regimes. The change in total cash reconciles all cash flow categories plus currency and other reconciling effects, but not every inflow is operating success.","A simplified free cash flow to firm starting point is operating cash flow less capital expenditures under a common analyst convention, although precise definitions vary, especially with interest classification, acquisitions, leases and working capital normalization. In the preceding scenario, if CFO is $70,000 and capital spending is $45,000, a basic CFO−capex FCF measure is $25,000. That $25,000 is not automatically cash payable to shareholders: debt service, acquisitions, working capital shocks and contractual restrictions can remain.","Suppose the issuer borrows an additional $80,000 and spends $60,000 buying back shares. End-of-year bank cash can increase from borrowing despite modest business FCF. An investor looking only at the higher cash account could infer improving operations incorrectly. Similarly a company can pay a dividend from debt or cash reserves for a time even when normal operations fail to cover distributions. Compare returns of capital with sustainable cash generation and capital requirements.","A careful model distinguishes maintenance capital expenditure needed to preserve the productive base from growth expenditure intended to create future returns, recognizing that filings may not separately identify them. EBITDA ignores capital expenditures, working capital and often certain fixed cash commitments. Evaluate cash conversion, economic return on invested capital, net borrowing and dilution together before declaring shareholders wealthy."],"worked":["Operating CFO $70,000 and capex $45,000 ⇒ simplified FCF $25,000.","New borrowing $80,000 is financing inflow, not sales revenue.","Repurchases $60,000 are financing outflow, not a deduction from net income.","CFO−capex FCF $25,000 does not equal all available distributable shareholder cash.","Repeat capex and debt maturity analysis before assuming long-term distribution capacity."],"practice":[["Why can cash in the bank grow even when operations are weak?","Borrowing, selling assets or issuing new shares can generate cash inflows independent of core operating performance; classify cash by its source."],["Why is a positive EBITDA figure inadequate evidence that all required debt payments are affordable?","EBITDA excludes capital expenditure, working-capital funding, interest, principal and tax obligations; those can consume much of the firm's cash."]]},{"title":"Balance-sheet liquidity, leverage and solvency stress","body":["Liquidity is the ability to meet near-term obligations, while solvency concerns the longer-term capacity of assets and cash flows to support all claims. Current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities; quick ratio restricts qualifying liquid assets. A current ratio above one is not a legal assurance of financial health because inventory could be difficult to sell, receivables may be overdue and a large debt maturity may fall just outside the classification period. Operating cash generation and committed credit availability supplement the snapshot.","Consider current assets $300,000 including $100,000 cash, $120,000 receivables and $80,000 inventory; current liabilities $200,000. Current ratio 1.5. A rough quick ratio using cash plus receivables is ($100,000+$120,000)/$200,000=1.1. If $60,000 of receivables become doubtful, true liquidity is worse than the unadjusted ratio implies. A company can also have apparently high liquidity because it just issued expensive short-term debt.","Leverage analysis compares debt with cash generation and asset collateral. Net debt can be defined as interest-bearing debt minus available cash, but restricted cash, leases and obligations require careful adjustments. Interest coverage based on EBIT divided by interest expense is an accrual metric, not a direct schedule of debt repayment capacity. A floating-rate loan repricing upward can weaken coverage even when EBIT is unchanged. Maturity ladders, covenants and refinancing conditions can determine survival in an economic slowdown.","Use at least two downside scenarios: a 15% revenue decline and a 2 percentage-point increase in borrowing cost. Trace operating earnings, collections, debt interest and capital spending rather than assume profitability falls proportionally. A research analyst should identify the maximum credible loss and the financing actions management might take, including dilutive issuance or asset sales."],"worked":["Current assets $300,000, current liabilities $200,000 ⇒ current ratio 1.50.","Cash $100,000 + receivables $120,000, excluding inventory $80,000 ⇒ simplified quick ratio 1.10.","If receivables of $60,000 are unrecoverable, liquid near-assets fall materially, even though the old ratio was 1.10.","Interest-bearing debt $250,000 less unrestricted cash $100,000 ⇒ net debt $150,000 under simple definition.","Interest coverage should be stress-tested against earnings and refinancing shocks."],"practice":[["Why is a current ratio of 1.5 insufficient evidence that a company can definitely pay every current bill?","Inventory and receivables may be illiquid or uncollectible, and debt maturities, covenants or seasonal cash needs can be more restrictive than the balance-sheet ratio."],["How can a company have positive shareholders' equity but still experience financial distress?","Book assets may not be readily liquidated at carrying value, bills can become due before cash arrives, and refinancing or covenant conditions may fail."]]},{"title":"Earnings quality, accrual estimates and financial reporting red flags","body":["Earnings quality evaluates whether reported accounting profit is supported by durable operations, sound recognition policies and eventual cash receipts. A rising earnings-per-share number can result from operating improvements, lower tax rate, share repurchases or a one-time asset sale. A single metric cannot distinguish those drivers. Reconcile net income, CFO, recurring capital investment and share count changes across multiple reports to identify why EPS changed.","Common analytical questions include receivables growth substantially above sales growth, inventory build despite slowing demand, unusually frequent restructuring add-backs, changes in useful-life depreciation estimates, gains on asset disposals, goodwill impairment indicators, aggressive acquisition accounting or negative operating cash in periods reporting strong profit. None is proof of fraud alone. Disclosures, audit qualifications, accounting policies and actual customer receipts are needed before alleging misconduct.","A simple accrual signal is net income minus operating cash flow, though noncash taxes, growth working capital and industry differences matter. In a fictional company reporting $200,000 net income and $120,000 CFO, the gap is $80,000. If capital expenditure is $70,000, FCF is only $50,000. Net income of $200,000 does not mean $200,000 of distributable cash and does not justify a dividend equal to all net income. Analysts should track change in receivables and working capital to explain the difference.","GAAP statements, auditor reports, footnotes and company claims of adjusted non-GAAP performance should be reconciled, not merely repeated. A reputable primary-source investment memo should mark all figures as reported, calculated or assumed; quantify adjustments; and disclose if the analyst could not reconcile an account. Financial analysis supports skepticism without presuming every difference between cash and earnings is wrongdoing."],"worked":["Company reports net income $200,000 and CFO $120,000 ⇒ net income−CFO gap $80,000.","Capital expenditures $70,000 ⇒ CFO−capex FCF $50,000.","Net income/FCF ratio =4.0× under this specific simplified definition.","If a $100,000 dividend is declared and paid, it exceeds modeled annual FCF by $50,000.","An observed mismatch is an investigatory signal, not a fraud determination."],"practice":[["What is one reason a profitable fast-growing manufacturer might report lower CFO than net income without fraud?","Growing receivables or inventory can use operating cash as output increases, so earnings recognition and customer collections differ in timing."],["Why can repeated 'adjusted earnings' exclusions warrant skepticism?","Costs called nonrecurring can occur regularly, and excluding actual compensation or restructuring obligations can make a reported profitability narrative overly optimistic."]]},{"title":"A fully reconciled fictional-company financial statement assignment","body":["Build a four-statement model with clearly identified start and end dates. Start with a balanced beginning balance sheet, record the transactions creating revenue and expenses, then reconcile ending net income through retained earnings and other equity changes. Explain operating CFO in terms of net income and every balance-sheet operating working-capital change. Calculate investing and financing cash flows independently and tie their sum to the movement in cash on the ending balance sheet.","An analyst should not plug the model with unexplained 'other' cash flows simply to force agreement. If cash does not reconcile, check whether currency, acquisitions, restricted cash or statement classification was overlooked. Likewise debt cannot disappear from the balance sheet when a company spends loan proceeds on a repurchase. Reported tax expense and actual tax cash paid may differ due to deferred taxes and working capital. Transparency about an unidentified difference is superior to inventing an accounting line.","Required output includes income statement margins, basic and diluted EPS assumptions, current and quick ratios, net debt, interest coverage, CFO−capex FCF, revenue and receivable growth, and the share count bridge. Compare two fiscal periods and explain whether cash conversion improved. Read issuer annual reports directly, with dated evidence of the fiscal year, filing amendments and whether the auditor expressed an unqualified or qualified opinion.","End with a written assessment: is the company generating sufficient recurring cash to preserve operations, finance debt and reward shareholders? What exact assumptions are necessary to recommend a valuation and what events would invalidate them? This closes the gap between elementary bookkeeping and professional investment decisions without using an attractive EPS alone as the answer."],"worked":["Hypothetical net income $90,000, depreciation $20,000, receivables increase $35,000, inventory increase $15,000, payables increase $10,000.","Calculate CFO $70,000; deduct $45,000 capex to obtain $25,000 simple FCF.","If current assets are $300,000 and liabilities $200,000, current ratio 1.5.","If shares are 30,000 weighted-average, simplified EPS $3 on net income $90,000 before attributable-profit adjustments.","A complete model must reconcile income, cash, debt and equity without unexplained balancing entries."],"practice":[["What is the strongest basic check that the three main statements were linked correctly?","Changes in balance-sheet cash should reconcile to reported operating, investing and financing cash movement after applicable reconciliation items, while net income updates equity under the proper rules."],["Why must an analyst report both cash flow and profitability before choosing whether a stock is undervalued?","Accrual profit, cash collections, investment requirements, leverage and share counts jointly determine possible shareholder cash returns and survival risk."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Beginners' Guide to Financial Statements","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/begfinstmtguide.htm"],["Investor.gov How to Read a 10-K","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/researching-investments/how-read-10-k"],["SEC EDGAR reporting documents","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"],["SEC Financial Reporting Manual","https://www.sec.gov/corpfin/cf-manual"],["FASB Accounting Standards Codification information","https://www.fasb.org/"],["Investor.gov Researching Investments","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/researching-investments"]],"assessment":{"title":"Four-statement earnings-quality and cash-conversion audit","prompt":"Reconstruct a fictional public issuer's revenue and earnings with $1m sales, $600k cost of revenue, $250k operating expense, $30k interest and $30k taxes. Build an indirect CFO reconciliation from $90k net income, $20k depreciation, $35k receivables increase, $15k inventory increase and $10k payables increase. Deduct $45k capex. Compute current/quick ratios, basic EPS and cash conversion. Explain what further notes and cash-flow items would be required to close a fully balanced four-statement model.","rubric":["Accounting equation and correctly classified statements (15%)","Income statement and EPS reconciliation (20%)","Indirect CFO and working-capital signs (25%)","Capital spending, liquidity and financing analysis (20%)","Documented earnings-quality skepticism and primary filings (20%)"]}},{"id":"L8.S3","title":"Valuing a Stock","subtitle":"Comparable multiples, discounted cash flows, WACC, terminal value and downside scenarios","prerequisites":["L7.S3 present value, NPV and rate units","L8.S1 market cap and enterprise value","L8.S2 operating cash and capital requirements"],"objectives":["Distinguish quoted price, market cap, enterprise value and intrinsic-value range","Reconcile EPS, P/E and normalized earnings with stock count","Construct revenue-margin-reinvestment cash-flow forecasts","Discount FCFF at appropriate WACC and bridge enterprise value to equity","Analyze terminal values and discount-rate sensitivity","Use price scenarios and risk capacity instead of falsely precise price targets"],"sections":[{"title":"Price is a transaction; valuation is a conditional model","body":["A quoted stock price is the amount at which a specific share can trade subject to spreads and order conditions; it is not an objective label of fair value. Intrinsic valuation estimates discounted future owner-relevant cash flows under explicit business and financing assumptions. Relative valuation compares pricing ratios with issuers that have sufficiently comparable business models, accounting, growth, risk and balance sheets. Both are vulnerable to bad assumptions. A stock can be inexpensive relative to its own history yet overvalued relative to future earnings capacity.","Separate price per share from total equity value. A $5 stock with two billion shares outstanding has a $10 billion market capitalization, whereas a $500 stock with one million shares has a $500 million cap. Share prices can change through splits without changing proportional ownership. The investor should ask what future cash flow and discount rate the current *market capitalization* implies, not whether one share costs less than a restaurant meal.","Margin of safety means comparing purchase price with a reasoned valuation under downside assumptions, not subtracting an arbitrary percentage from a hopeful target. If estimated equity value is $600 million and 12 million fully relevant shares are expected, implied modeled share value is $50. Paying $40 implies a 20% discount to that $50 model value, but the discount disappears if the business value falls or expected dilution grows. The price gap does not guarantee a profitable outcome.","Construct a hypothesis table: current quotation, forecast fundamental value range, major drivers, disconfirming evidence, transaction costs, tax considerations and how a drawdown affects household liquidity. A discounted cash-flow model should produce a decision framework, not a precise prophecy. Disclosing why a forecast might be wrong is part of sound equity research."],"worked":["Company A 2bn shares at $5 = $10bn capitalization; B 1m shares at $500 = $500m.","Modeled equity $600m divided by 12m shares ⇒ hypothetical $50 share value.","Price $40 versus modeled $50 ⇒ discount to modeled value (50−40)/50 =20%.","If modeled value falls to $35, a $40 purchase is no longer below modeled value.","Share splits do not multiply total economic value or compensate for business risk."],"practice":[["Why is a $5 stock not inherently cheaper than a $500 stock?","The economic cost of the whole business depends on share count, debt, cash and future fundamentals; nominal price per share is not total valuation."],["What makes a valuation range more defensible than a single 'fair price' forecast?","Revenue, margins, reinvestment, discount rates and dilution are uncertain, and scenario ranges reveal how strongly value depends on those assumptions."]]},{"title":"P/E, earnings yield, EV/EBITDA and denominator quality","body":["Price-to-earnings is share price divided by earnings per share, or market capitalization divided by net income attributable to the relevant common equity, if the numerator and denominator align. Earnings yield is the reciprocal of a positive P/E, not a guaranteed distribution rate or bond coupon. With share price $60 and annual EPS $3, P/E equals 20× and earnings yield 5%. If EPS is temporarily inflated by one-time gains, the reported multiple understates the true recurring operating valuation.","Enterprise multiples such as EV/EBITDA compare enterprise value with a pre-interest earnings proxy, useful in some industry comparisons. EBITDA excludes depreciation and amortization, which may represent real long-term capital consumption, and usually does not capture capital expenditures or working-capital requirements. Banks, insurers, REITs, utilities and early-growth companies often require different measurement choices. A company with negative EPS can have an algebraically negative P/E but that is not a meaningful comparable cheapness metric.","Normalize earnings by separating recurring operating performance from transitory gains, unusually low tax expense, acquisition accounting, restructuring expenses and cyclically extreme margins. Analysts should not simply subtract expenses they dislike; a recurring employee compensation bill is a real economic cost even if paid in shares. Compare net income, free cash flow, invested capital, leverage and share-count history when deciding whether an earnings multiple is appropriate.","Use at least three peers chosen because products, margins, capital intensity, debt structures and growth stages are actually comparable. A company priced at 10× earnings may deserve the lower multiple if profits are deteriorating, credit risk is elevated or its intellectual property is expiring. Relative multiples can anchor a disciplined cross-check but cannot substitute for cash-flow modeling."],"worked":["Stock price $60 / EPS $3 ⇒ P/E 20×.","Earnings yield $3/$60=5%, not a guaranteed cash return.","If normalized recurring EPS is $2.40 rather than reported $3, normalized P/E=$60/$2.40=25×.","Simplified EV $500m / EBITDA $50m =10×.","A ratio with a mismatched capital claim and earnings measure is not economically valid."],"practice":[["Does a 5% earnings yield from a 20× P/E promise the stockholder a 5% cash distribution?","No. Earnings are accounting results, not necessarily distributable cash, and future profits and dividends can differ from those reported."],["Why should a firm with frequent 'one-time' restructuring charges be evaluated beyond adjusted EPS?","Recurring exclusions can mask ongoing costs and dilute the reliability of normalized margins; cash and reported GAAP figures should be reconciled."]]},{"title":"Build cash flows from operations, margins and reinvestment","body":["Discounted cash flow begins with operating assumptions, not an unexplained future stock price. Forecast sales from addressable market, volume, price, retention and realistic competition, then estimate operating profit, cash tax rate, reinvestment and working-capital changes. Under a common enterprise-valuation convention, free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) is EBIT×(1−tax rate)+depreciation/amortization−capital expenditures−increase in net operating working capital, subject to classification and lease/other adjustments.","Suppose annual revenue is $100 million, operating EBIT margin 20%, cash tax assumption 25%, depreciation $4 million, capital expenditure $9 million and increase in operating working capital $3 million. EBIT is $20 million, tax-adjusted operating income $15 million and FCFF=$15m+$4m−$9m−$3m=$7 million. A company reporting $20 million EBIT does not create $20 million of annual owner-available cash. Positive growth can require working capital and equipment before the cash arrives.","Historical financial statements provide evidence for revenue growth and reinvestment ratios, but not certainty about the next decade. Analyst forecasts should reconcile capital expenditures with growth and maintenance needs, depreciation with the asset base and margins with competitors. Forecasts showing revenue double while investments and working capital remain fixed need explicit justification. Taxes require modeled economic treatment rather than blindly applying the household's ordinary income-tax bracket.","Scenario construction combines volume, pricing, labor cost, working capital and capital needs. A downside model may hold revenue flat, reduce operating margin to 15% and increase inventory investment, while an upside model assumes sustainable productivity improvements. Evaluate company behavior under stress and distinguish operations from financing. The valuation equation cannot rescue a model built on economically impossible cash-flow assumptions."],"worked":["Revenue $100m with EBIT margin 20% ⇒ EBIT $20m.","Tax 25% on operating profit ⇒ NOPAT $15m.","Add D&A $4m, subtract capex $9m and working-capital investment $3m.","FCFF=$15m+$4m−$9m−$3m=$7m.","Revenue growth requiring increased inventory can reduce free cash flow even during profit expansion."],"practice":[["Why is EBIT not the cash amount available for all investors?","Cash taxes, capital spending and changes in operating working capital must be modeled; reported EBIT is accrual profit before these cash allocations."],["What would make a forecast of high sales growth without any additional reinvestment suspect?","More production, inventory, receivables or productive capacity often requires capital; a no-investment assumption needs specific evidence of unusual scalable economics."]]},{"title":"Discount rates: the debt/equity capital structure and WACC","body":["Enterprise free cash flows are commonly discounted using a weighted average cost of capital (WACC) consistent with the financing and tax assumptions. Simplified WACC=(E/V)×cost of equity+(D/V)×pretax cost of debt×(1−marginal corporate tax rate), where V=E+D for the simple two-claim case. Actual financing can include preferred shares, operating leases, debt market values and changing leverage. The estimated cost of equity reflects risk and opportunity cost, not the dividend yield or an average bank deposit APY.","Consider $600 million market equity financing and $400 million market debt financing. With cost of equity 10%, pretax debt cost 6% and a 25% illustrative cash tax shield rate, simplified WACC is .60×.10+.40×.06×.75=.078, or 7.8%. Discounting enterprise FCFF at the equity cost of 10% would be inconsistent with this simplified structure. Conversely, discounting a dividend or FCFE stream at 7.8% could inappropriately blend debt costs into cash flows already available only after debt financing.","Cost of capital estimates are uncertain. A capital asset pricing model may represent required equity return as a risk-free yield plus beta times an assumed equity risk premium, but beta changes across periods and does not capture every important downside. A highly levered firm may need a more complex approach when debt costs and tax shields shift with financial distress. A 1-percentage-point change in WACC can substantially alter terminal values, particularly for businesses expected to generate cash far in the future.","The essential discipline is matching the cash-flow claimant to its discount rate and keeping nominal versus real treatment consistent. Nominal dollars projected with price growth need a nominal cost of capital. An analyst should provide a source-dated rate range and sensitivity table rather than a single precise WACC invented to justify today's share price."],"worked":["Equity share 60% at cost 10% ⇒ contribution to WACC 6%.","Debt share 40% at pretax cost 6%, tax rate 25% ⇒ contribution 1.8%.","Simplified WACC 6%+1.8%=7.8%.","A 1-point WACC change can substantially change long-duration cash-flow valuation.","FCFF uses enterprise-consistent discount rate; FCFE and dividends use equity-consistent required return."],"practice":[["Why is a company's common dividend yield not automatically the appropriate discount rate for enterprise FCFF?","Dividend yield is one observed distribution divided by price, while FCFF belongs to multiple capital providers and WACC reflects the assumed costs and weights of their claims."],["What important limitation surrounds a WACC calculated from historical accounting debt and equity?","Market value weights and current financing costs may differ from historical book values; the economic capital structure and projected risk require evidence."]]},{"title":"Terminal value, perpetual growth and severe model sensitivity","body":["Forecasting every future business year explicitly is impractical, so many DCFs use a finite forecast horizon plus a terminal value. Under a continuing-value model with first post-forecast FCFF FCF_(N+1), perpetual growth g and discount rate WACC greater than g, TV_N=FCF_(N+1)/(WACC−g). Terminal value at the horizon must then be discounted back N periods to today along with finite-period cash flows. The perpetuity assumes sustainable long-term growth and reinvestment, not an arbitrary number chosen to make a preferred price target.","For example, FCFF expected just after year five is $10 million, WACC 8% and terminal growth 3%. The terminal value at year five is $10m/(.08−.03)=$200 million. At 9% WACC with unchanged growth, value is $166.67 million; at 8% WACC with 4% growth, value jumps to $250 million. These are *horizon-date* values and cannot be directly combined with today's share price without discounting them and all interim cash flows.","Large terminal-value shares of enterprise value reveal dependence on distant assumptions. The firm may have little current FCF but a DCF can still produce an enormous value if future margin and growth assumptions are optimistic. When growth approaches the discount rate, denominator sensitivity becomes extreme; if g≥WACC, the constant-growth perpetuity is invalid. A disciplined model checks market size, inflation, economic reinvestment and whether margins can persist indefinitely.","Use a sensitivity matrix across WACC and terminal growth, plus explicit annual free-cash-flow paths in a bear, base and bull case. State which scenarios are economically plausible, not merely mathematically possible. Many attractive investment stories are less robust after considering dilution, debt claims, future capital expenditures and the chance that terminal assumptions fail."],"worked":["Next post-forecast FCFF $10m, WACC 8%, growth 3%.","Year-five terminal value $10m/(.08−.03)=$200m.","WACC 9%, same growth ⇒ terminal value $166.67m.","WACC 8%, growth 4% ⇒ terminal value $250m.","Each terminal value requires a further 5-year discount factor to compare with today's enterprise value."],"practice":[["Why is a terminal value at the end of year five not already a present-day enterprise value?","It prices continuing cash flow at the forecast-horizon date; that future value must be discounted to today and added to the PV of interim cash flows."],["What warning does a terminal-growth assumption very close to WACC create?","The value is extremely sensitive and the perpetuity may become economically implausible or mathematically invalid as g approaches the discount rate."]]},{"title":"From enterprise value to equity price with cash, debt and dilution","body":["An enterprise DCF first values cash flow available across financing claims. Bridge enterprise value to common equity using debt and other claims and appropriate cash adjustments; the simplified relation is equity value=enterprise value−debt+excess cash. Then divide by a justified current or forecast diluted share count. A DCF that omits expected future employee share awards or new financing can systematically overstate per-share value even when its enterprise value is otherwise plausible.","Suppose an enterprise valuation produces $500 million, with $150 million net interest-bearing debt and 10 million expected economically relevant diluted shares. Simplified equity valuation is $350 million, or $35 per share. At an illustrative $40 market price, the market trades about 14.29% above that model estimate. If the model mistakenly divides by 8 million shares, it reports $43.75, flipping the buy-or-avoid conclusion. Share count and debt treatment are material valuation inputs, not footnotes.","An individual company has costs and uncertain cash flows; a household also has an investment budget and time horizon. A $35 modeled estimate does not protect an investor paying $30 from a collapse in earnings or a new securities issuance. Compare the current quote with a range of defensible values and calculate downside exposure after costs and taxes. One stock should not displace an emergency reserve or money committed to a known mortgage closing date.","Provide a final valuation memo with a reconciled historical cash flow table, 3–5 years of forecast drivers, tax assumptions, terminal mechanism, WACC, debt/cash/share bridge and a price sensitivity. Identify a decision condition that would invalidate the thesis. The professional outcome may be 'insufficient evidence' when underlying disclosure quality is weak, not a manufactured positive recommendation."],"worked":["DCF enterprise value $500m − net debt $150m = equity value $350m.","Correct diluted shares 10m ⇒ modeled share value $35.","Mistaken share count 8m ⇒ incorrect $43.75 share value.","Quoted market price $40 is about 14.29% above the $35 valuation in this illustration.","The value range must also reflect operating and tax uncertainty, not only share count."],"practice":[["Why can an otherwise correct enterprise DCF still overvalue each common share?","Failure to subtract senior financial claims or use an appropriate diluted share count overstates residual per-share equity value."],["Why can a well-researched model valuation below the market price be a rational no-buy decision?","The model's expected compensation for business and market risk may be insufficient at the quote, particularly if downside scenarios are material to the investor's goals."]]},{"title":"Investment committee recommendation with scenarios and counterevidence","body":["Finish a disciplined company valuation by writing three internally coherent forecasts, each including revenue, EBIT margin, cash taxes, capital expenditure, working capital, terminal rate and relevant share count. Price is an output, not an input used to manipulate assumptions. Show at least one independent comparable-company cross-check and reconcile different accounting definitions when businesses use different fiscal years or non-GAAP measures.","Stress tests should connect to specific risks. A manufacturer with rising inventory could face lower demand, price discounting, lower margins and reduced CFO simultaneously. A software company might scale with modest tangible capex yet incur large stock-based compensation and continuing employee dilution. A highly indebted distributor may face rising interest expense during a recession. A generic 10% discount on all cash flows cannot substitute for understanding the actual operating mechanism.","Position sizing is separate from business valuation. A potentially undervalued issuer can still be inappropriate for a family whose money is needed in six months; a 30% drawdown on $5,000 would create a $1,500 cash shortfall. A long-term portfolio should have explicit diversification, taxes, trading costs and liquidity requirements. Backtesting a stock thesis after the fact is subject to hindsight and selective evidence; use dated filings and record what facts were available at the original decision time.","The deliverable must name the company or state that it is fictional, specify source dates and definitions, show calculations and explain a disconfirming fact. Students should learn when to reject an investment despite the excitement of a rising chart. That skill is the difference between financial literacy and confident speculation."],"worked":["Hypothetical modeled value $35 per share and market quote $40 ⇒ quote exceeds model value.","Potential 30% decline on $5,000 concentrated position loses $1,500.","If financing increases debt claims $100m, equity bridge changes even if enterprise forecast is unchanged.","A price target with no explicit forecast free-cash-flow path is not auditable.","Investment committee must state evidence, dissenting case and a no-investment option."],"practice":[["What does it mean for an equity research thesis to be falsifiable?","The analyst specifies measurable observations such as sustained falling cash margins, a debt maturity failure or material dilution that would refute the assumptions behind valuation."],["Why is company valuation not the same decision as portfolio allocation?","A security might have attractive expected value yet expose a household to unacceptable concentration, short-horizon loss or liquidity risk relative to required obligations."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC EDGAR and issuer financial statements","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"],["Investor.gov How to Read a 10-K","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/researching-investments/how-read-10-k"],["SEC Beginners' Guide to Financial Statements","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/begfinstmtguide.htm"],["Investor.gov Stocks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks"],["CFA Institute Financial Statement Analysis curricula","https://www.cfainstitute.org/programs/cfa-program/candidate-resources/level-i-exam"]],"assessment":{"title":"Enterprise value, discounted cash flow and equity valuation","prompt":"Using fictional $100m annual revenue at 20% EBIT margin, 25% tax, $4m depreciation, $9m capex and $3m working capital increase, derive FCFF. Compute 60/40 WACC with 10% cost of equity and 6% pretax debt. Model $10m horizon-date terminal FCFF under 8% required rate and 3% and 4% perpetual growth. Bridge $500m enterprise value to $350m common equity with $150m net debt and compare per-share value under 8m versus 10m diluted shares. Cross-check against normalized P/E and report three material failure conditions.","rubric":["Correct valuation claim/metric matching (20%)","FCFF and capital spending forecast (20%)","WACC and discount rate consistency (20%)","Terminal and diluted equity sensitivity (25%)","Counterevidence, source quality and risk judgment (15%)"]}},{"id":"L8.S4","title":"Dividends, Buybacks and Returns","subtitle":"Total shareholder return, payout sustainability, reinvestment, repurchases, dilution and taxes","prerequisites":["L8.S1 equity claims and capital structure","L8.S2 cash flow, retained earnings and FCF","L8.S3 enterprise and per-share value"],"objectives":["Calculate shareholder holding-period return and dividend yield correctly","Separate cash distributions from ex-dividend price adjustments","Evaluate dividend coverage with earnings, cash and debt obligations","Calculate effects of repurchases on EPS, cash, leverage and fair value","Interpret dilution, stock compensation and net buyback share counts","Compare reinvestment, debt retirement, dividends and buybacks under different assumptions","Construct after-tax investor and household-goal allocation scenarios"],"sections":[{"title":"What shareholder return actually measures","body":["Shareholder return over a specified period combines price change with distributions received, and may be adjusted for reinvestment, fees, taxes and corporate actions. A basic simple holding-period return on one share is (P_end−P_start+cash distributions per share)/P_start. It is important to identify the interval and whether dividends are counted in cash or reinvested. Price return alone understates the economic return on a dividend-paying security, while annualized percentages cannot be compared indiscriminately with one-month returns.","A fictional share begins at $50, ends at $54 and pays $1.50 cash dividend during the period. Capital price gain is $4 or 8%; combined gross return is $5.50/$50=11%. That 11% is not automatically an annual return if the interval is three months, and it does not guarantee the same return in the next period. If commissions and bid-ask spreads reduce ending proceeds, economic return is lower. If the dividend is taxable, net spendable return can be lower still.","Reinvestment changes the number of shares and future compounding; calculations need ex-dividend and reinvestment dates. A total-return index may assume dividends reinvested and ignore individual tax liabilities. A price-only chart and a total-return chart can make the same issuer look different over long horizons. A stock split changes share count and split-adjusted prices without increasing economic corporate wealth; analysts should compare split-adjusted series.","Written analysis should distinguish the security's return from the investor's money-weighted experience after contributions and withdrawals. Buying more shares at a high price can reduce the actual investor's IRR despite a positive multi-year index return. A household needing mortgage closing cash must use its own dates and money at risk rather than the headline annual performance number."],"worked":["Buy at $50, end at $54, cash dividend $1.50.","Price return ($54−$50)/$50=8%.","Total return ($54−$50+$1.50)/$50=11% before tax and costs.","Two years +20% then −20% ⇒ cumulative factor 1.2×.8=.96, total −4%.","Separate money-weighted investor results from a benchmark's time-weighted record."],"practice":[["How is price return different from holding-period total return?","Price return includes only change in market price, while total return also includes distributions received over the stated period before specified costs or taxes."],["Why is a three-month 11% return not itself an 11% annual return?","The measurement interval differs; annualization needs an explicit compounding convention and does not predict future performance."]]},{"title":"Dividend declaration, record, ex-dividend and payment mechanics","body":["Dividends are corporate distributions declared under applicable governance and solvency rules. Common cash dividends are not automatically owed before declaration. The board chooses how much to retain or pay subject to corporate law, debt covenants, regulatory requirements and liquidity. A shareholder's entitlement follows the security's ex-dividend, record and payment mechanics under applicable market and corporate action rules; investors should verify the official dividend announcement and exchange conventions.","A common misconception is that buying immediately before ex-dividend creates free economic income. Other things equal, the share price tends to adjust downward by roughly the distribution amount when the stock trades ex-dividend, because cash leaves corporate assets; actual prices also respond to market news and tax effects. A $50 stock paying $1 could theoretically open around $49 absent all other changes. Receiving $1 while the price drops $1 does not create $1 new wealth. Tax consequences and transaction costs can make such timing worse.","Dividend yield is annual declared or indicated dividends per share divided by current share price, and is not a promised bank interest rate. If annual indicated payment is $2 and price falls from $50 to $25, the trailing yield appears to rise from 4% to 8%; that could signal a future cut rather than a bargain. Dividends can be reduced, suspended or funded unsustainably through debt or asset sales.","Company filings explain restricted payments, retained earnings and dividend policy. Market data should distinguish trailing dividends, announced future payments, regular versus special dividends, and stock distributions. A professional memo should reconcile payment frequency and denominators rather than multiply an unusual special dividend by four to invent an ongoing annual yield."],"worked":["Annual indicated cash dividend $2/share, price $50 ⇒ indicated yield 4%.","If quote declines to $25 with unchanged historical annual payment, arithmetic trailing yield becomes 8%.","Ex-dividend illustrative $50 pre-ex price minus $1 dividend ≈$49 other things equal.","Shareholder may receive $1 and hold share worth $49, total ~$50 before taxes and market changes.","An 8% quoted trailing yield is not a promise that the board maintains $2 annual cash."],"practice":[["Why does a dividend distribution not guarantee an immediate increase in shareholder wealth by the cash amount?","Corporate cash decreases when paid and market prices generally adjust for the distribution, with other market effects superimposed."],["What should a student confirm before treating an unusually high dividend yield as sustainable income?","Check whether the dividend is special or recurring, future board decisions, earnings and FCF coverage, debt covenants and market-price causes."]]},{"title":"Dividend payout ratios, cash coverage and balance-sheet constraints","body":["Dividend payout ratio may be calculated as dividends divided by net earnings, or per-share dividend divided by EPS, when amounts are aligned. The FCF payout ratio uses dividends relative to an appropriately defined free-cash-flow measure. Differences matter because cash conversion and capex requirements can cause net income to exceed spendable funds. Companies with negative earnings and dividend payments require a different analysis; a nonsensical negative payout ratio does not establish sustainability.","Suppose a fictional firm reports net income $200 million, CFO $120 million and capital expenditures $70 million. Simplified free cash flow is $50 million. If it pays $40 million cash dividends, earnings payout is 20%, but FCF payout is 80%. If it pays $80 million dividends, earnings payout is only 40% while FCF payout is 160%, requiring cash reserves, debt, asset sales or a temporary deviation from normal cash generation. A low earnings payout can hide a high cash burden.","Debt maturities, working-capital investment and recession variability affect coverage. A company can pay dividends for several years even after profitability deteriorates, especially if it has a large cash hoard. That does not mean each dividend is financed by sustainable operations. An issuer should retain sufficient funds for safe operations, debt obligations and financially attractive projects before distributions, subject to governance and contractual constraints.","Estimate a stress payout: if FCF falls 50% from $50m to $25m while dividends stay $40m, cash coverage falls to 0.625× and cash shortfall becomes $15m. If the business also owes principal on debt, the pressure may be worse. The student's conclusion should assess flexibility, not label an issuer safe merely because it paid dividends for decades."],"worked":["Net income $200m; CFO $120m; capex $70m ⇒ FCF $50m.","Cash dividend $40m ⇒ earnings payout $40m/$200m=20%.","FCF dividend payout $40m/$50m=80%.","FCF stress $25m with unchanged dividend $40m ⇒ payout ratio 160% and cash gap $15m.","Dividend policy must be judged against cash generation, debt and future investment."],"practice":[["Why can a low earnings payout ratio mask a risky dividend policy?","Net income may not convert to free cash after working-capital and capex needs; the cash distribution can exceed sustainable FCF despite a low ratio to earnings."],["Does a dividend payout ratio above 100% prove immediate insolvency?","No. Cash reserves or financing may temporarily fund a distribution, but the source and long-run sustainability need investigation."]]},{"title":"Buyback math: company cash, shares and EPS are a complete system","body":["Repurchases use corporate funds to acquire shares, potentially reducing shares outstanding and increasing each remaining share's percentage of the company. A buyback is not automatically an economic gain: the company gives up cash, and buying shares at an excessive price can transfer value from continuing holders to departing sellers. A repurchase may also offset stock-based compensation dilution without producing a net reduction in shares. Analyze the exact executed cash outlay and outstanding count, not a press release authorizing a large maximum program.","Take a hypothetical company with 10 million shares, $100 million earnings and a $20 share price. Before repurchase, EPS=$10. If the company uses $20 million cash to buy one million shares at $20, the gross count falls to nine million, and with *unchanged* earnings EPS rises to $11.11. Yet cash fell by $20 million and future interest income or operating investment capacity may change; the unchanged-earnings premise is an explicit simplification. The enterprise and common equity value must account for the lost cash.","If stock is worth less than management pays, remaining shareholders may be disadvantaged. Imagine economic equity value $300m immediately before an overpriced $40m buyback at $40/share, with 10m shares; the fair pre-buyback estimate is $30/share. After buying one million shares and reducing corporate cash/equity value by $40m, fair equity becomes $260m divided by 9m = $28.89 per remaining share. The buyback increased reported ownership percentage but reduced hypothetical per-share intrinsic value. Conversely, repurchasing below value can be beneficial under correct assumptions.","Real repurchases occur across dates and prices and influence weighted-average shares gradually. SEC filings include executed issuer purchase tables and board authorization. Analysts should reconcile employee share issuance, actual repurchases and average diluted shares, plus compare foregone debt repayment or projects. Buybacks are capital allocation choices, not free share-price engineering."],"worked":["10m shares, $100m earnings ⇒ EPS $10.","Buy 1m shares at $20 ⇒ cash paid $20m, share count 9m.","If earnings unchanged, simple EPS rises to $100m/9m≈$11.11.","Separate intrinsic illustration: value $300m initially /10m=$30; buy 1m at $40 ⇒ remaining value $260m/9m≈$28.89.","An EPS increase can coexist with lower intrinsic economic value per remaining share."],"practice":[["Why can EPS rise after a repurchase even if the company's operations do not improve?","The same earnings may be divided by fewer weighted-average shares; cash is spent and future earnings/capital structure may also change."],["Can buying back shares above modeled intrinsic value harm continuing holders?","Yes. The company pays away more cash per retired share than the modeled value, reducing economic value remaining for each continuing share under the example assumptions."]]},{"title":"Stock compensation, dilution and net shareholder yield","body":["Stock-based compensation can align employees with owners but carries an economic cost. Issuing new shares, options or restricted stock units may dilute existing shareholders, while treasury repurchases might partly or completely offset that issuance. The net change in fully relevant shares affects ownership and per-share financial results. A corporation reporting large gross buybacks is not necessarily returning comparable economic value if it simultaneously issues shares to employees or acquires companies using stock.","Suppose an issuer starts with 100 million shares, repurchases 5 million and grants/exercises 3 million new shares over the period, with all transactions affecting the relevant outstanding count. End count is 98 million, a 2% net reduction rather than 5%. If share price is $20, gross buyback cost could be $100m assuming executions at that price; issuance proceeds may differ from the share value of compensation granted. Analysts must inspect cash payments, grant fair value, tax effects and weighted-average share disclosure to quantify the cost.","Shareholder yield is sometimes described as dividend yield plus buyback yield minus net issuance, but conventions vary substantially. Gross repurchase yield is not the same as net share reduction or a guaranteed investor return. A firm can raise debt to pay a dividend and buy shares, increasing reported distribution yield while increasing financial risk. Comparisons must use explicit share count and financing logic.","Track net dilution across several years alongside stock-based compensation as a proportion of revenue and operating cash flow. When estimating future price per share, ensure the denominator is consistent with anticipated issuance. A strong firm may still deliver weak shareholder returns if compensation dilution, ill-advised acquisitions or excessive debt cancel operating value creation."],"worked":["Starting shares 100m; repurchases 5m; new issued shares 3m ⇒ ending 98m.","Gross retired percentage 5%, net reduction 2% of initial shares.","If all 5m purchased at $20, cash spent $100m before costs.","A simple price-times-issuance count need not equal accounting expense or cash proceeds from employee grants.","A valuation must reflect both retired shares and newly issued economic interests."],"practice":[["Why might a firm advertising a 5% buyback yield reduce outstanding shares by only 2%?","New shares issued through employee awards, acquisitions or other financing offset some of the shares bought back."],["What is wrong with treating gross buyback cash outflow as a guaranteed additional stockholder return?","Repurchases reduce company cash and may increase financing risk; per-share economic effects depend on price paid, dilution, operating performance and tax treatment."]]},{"title":"Capital allocation: retain, reinvest, repay debt or distribute","body":["Managers can allocate operating cash among maintenance spending, new projects, paying down debt, cash reserves, dividends and repurchases. A disciplined capital allocation framework compares the incremental risk-adjusted present value of each option, rather than prioritizing dividends merely because they produce visible monthly income. A business with profitable capacity investments may compound shareholder value by reinvesting; another with declining opportunities may do better returning cash or reducing expensive debt.","Use return on invested capital (ROIC), broadly after-tax operating profit divided by appropriately defined invested capital, while acknowledging that invested-capital conventions vary. If NOPAT is $15m and average invested capital $150m, ROIC=10%. If the business's properly estimated WACC is 8%, the spread is +2 percentage points. However, average current ROIC is not proof new projects will earn 10%; incremental reinvestment return matters. Destructive expansion can turn a successful past operating history into disappointing future cash flows.","Debt repayment provides an often more predictable reduction of contractual interest costs than equity investments offer in forecast return. A firm repaying $20m of variable debt at 7% might save $1.4m pretax interest per year if rates and terms remain as modeled. After-tax economic cost, lost liquidity and debt covenants alter the actual benefit. Management must retain adequate cash for payroll, inventory and economic downturns.","A student capital allocation memo should compare at least four uses of $20m in hypothetical discretionary company cash: 1) reinvest in a project with positive risk-adjusted NPV, 2) retire 7% debt, 3) pay dividends, or 4) repurchase shares only at a justified price. Estimate effect on future free cash flow, financial risk, dilution and per-share value. A decision can be conditional: repurchases below intrinsic value, debt repayment during high leverage, reinvestment where competitive returns persist."],"worked":["NOPAT $15m and average invested capital $150m ⇒ ROIC 10%.","Illustrative WACC 8%, simple ROIC spread +2 percentage points.","Repay $20m variable debt at 7% ⇒ approximate $1.4m annual pretax interest reduction.","A positive ROIC-WACC spread on historical assets does not guarantee positive future projects.","Choose cash allocation based on future marginal value and risk rather than dividend appearance alone."],"practice":[["Why might retaining earnings be better for shareholders than paying all earnings as dividends?","If management has credible positive-NPV investments and sufficient governance, reinvested cash can create more long-run value than immediate distribution, though execution remains uncertain."],["What key distinction must be made when using a company's historical ROIC to justify new projects?","Historic average profitability is not the incremental return on new investment; the relevant forecast must evaluate new projects' marginal returns and financing needs."]]},{"title":"Tax-aware, risk-aware shareholder distributions and a research memo","body":["Investors hold stock in different account wrappers and tax jurisdictions. Qualified dividend rules, nonqualified distributions, long-term capital gains, net investment income taxes, state taxes and basis adjustments can affect after-tax wealth. Reinvesting dividends in a taxable account usually does not eliminate reportable dividend income and generally creates basis in additional acquired shares. Share buybacks may defer taxable realization for a continuing shareholder but do not eliminate all possible future capital-gain taxes or ensure a better after-tax outcome.","Consider a fictional owner with 100 shares originally purchased at $50 who receives $1.50 per share in a taxable account. Gross dividend cash is $150; whether it is qualified and the applicable federal and Connecticut tax consequences depend on documented facts. Automatically reinvesting $150 generally creates a new tax-lot basis for shares acquired, while the dividend may still be reportable. A second owner holding the same stock in a qualifying Roth account faces different rules but equal underlying market risk.","Rebalance household goals before concluding a high-yield issuer is appropriate. If a family needs $12,000 cash for mortgage closing in a year, a stock paying a 5% dividend is not a 5% insured bank deposit. A 30% stock price drawdown can overwhelm multiple years of cash dividends and threaten the transaction. Equity ownership belongs in a portfolio sized for risk capacity and time horizon, after emergency reserves and near-term obligations are addressed.","Final memo: identify the issuer and filing date, calculate total return and dividend sustainability, reconcile repurchases and issuance, estimate ROIC and required reinvestment, explain account tax classification, and state a base, upside and downside capital-allocation strategy. The learner must present a reasoned recommendation with an explicit decline-to-invest alternative and document information that would change the decision."],"worked":["100 shares ×$1.50 dividend = $150 gross cash distribution.","At $50 initial price, 100 shares originally cost $5,000; distribution alone equals 3% of that original cost for one period.","If shares later fall 30% on $5,000 starting value, price decline $1,500 outweighs $150 cash dividend.","Reinvested $150 can generate new share tax lot basis while remaining taxable in a standard brokerage under applicable rules.","After-tax result requires actual tax status and legal distribution classification, not an invented flat dividend tax."],"practice":[["Why isn't a high dividend-paying stock a cash-equivalent substitute for a home down payment fund?","Equity market value and distributions are uncertain; a drawdown may exceed all dividends and make funds unavailable at the required closing date."],["Why is automatically reinvesting dividends not necessarily a strategy to avoid current tax in a taxable account?","Taxable dividends may remain reportable even when reinvested, while the reinvested amount generally becomes basis in newly acquired shares."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC EDGAR company repurchase and dividend disclosures","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"],["SEC investor basics on stocks and dividends","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks"],["SEC Guide to Financial Statements and cash sources","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/begfinstmtguide.htm"],["IRS Publication 550 investment income and distributions","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Publication 551 basis and stock transactions","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551"],["FINRA Investing in Stocks","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/stocks"]],"assessment":{"title":"Total shareholder return and board capital-allocation case","prompt":"Calculate a one-period $50→$54 stock with $1.50 dividend; compare 20% net-income payout and 80% FCF payout for a fictional $200m earnings, $120m CFO, $70m capex and $40m dividend. Stress FCF −50%. Analyze a $20m repurchase of 1m shares at $20 for an issuer with 10m original shares and $100m net income, then demonstrate why a buyback at $40 when fair equity $300m can lower remaining per-share value. Reconcile 5m repurchases and 3m new issues at a 100m-share firm. Compare dividend, debt repayment and positive-NPV reinvestment from an after-tax and downside perspective.","rubric":["Correct dividend and total-return math (20%)","Cash payout sustainability and FCF stress (20%)","Buybacks, cash effects, share counts and fair value (25%)","Tax, capital allocation and shareholder dilution (20%)","Investment policy, counterevidence and source documentation (15%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"University-Style Company Analysis and Household Investment Committee","intro":"Research, accounting and capital allocation are evaluated together. Fictional Company Alder Industries is not an actual security, ticker, SEC registrant, current investment recommendation or investment price quote. Use every stated assumption, separate observed from modeled figures, and explain risks to a Connecticut household preparing for a future mortgage.","scenario":["Alder Industries has ten million original common shares outstanding at a hypothetical $40 market price. It considers a primary issuance of two million additional shares at $20, before underwriting fees. A current holder owns 100,000 shares. A different simplified capital-structure illustration gives pre-transaction equity market capitalization $400 million, $150 million interest-bearing debt and $50 million excess cash.","The company is reported in an educational illustration with $1,000,000 annual sales, $600,000 cost of sales, $250,000 operating expenses, $30,000 interest and $30,000 current income tax. Basic weighted-average shares in this small-scale income-statement exercise are 30,000; this is a separate fictional sample from the 10-million-share issuer and must not be mixed without scaling.","That financial-statement sample has net income $90,000, depreciation $20,000, receivables growth $35,000, inventory growth $15,000 and supplier payable growth $10,000. It buys $45,000 in capital equipment. Current assets $300,000 include $100,000 cash, $120,000 receivables and $80,000 inventory, and current liabilities are $200,000.","Alder's enterprise-level operating model separately assumes $100 million annual revenue, 20% EBIT margin, 25% operating cash tax assumption, $4 million D&A, $9 million capex and $3 million increase in operating working capital. Equity market weight is 60%, debt weight 40%, cost of common equity 10%, pretax debt cost 6% and deductible debt cost tax factor based on 25%.","A projected cash flow at the end of forecast year five has first following-year FCFF $10 million, continuing growth 3%, and assumed terminal discount rate 8%. Analysts must separately stress 9% discount and 4% continuing growth. The terminal value is computed at year five and cannot be treated as present enterprise value.","A fictional enterprise valuation is independently estimated at $500 million, with $150 million net debt and ten million economically relevant fully diluted shares. A junior analyst accidentally divides the residual equity value by eight million shares. Current market quote is a separate hypothetical $40.","A second shareholder return case begins at $50/share, ends at $54/share and pays $1.50/share in the period. Separately, a board reports $200 million annual net earnings, $120 million operating cash and $70 million capex, and considers cash dividends of $40 million.","An example of mechanical repurchase reports ten million shares, $100 million earnings, and a purchase of one million shares for $20 million at $20/share. A separate fair-value counterexample values ten million shares at $300 million but repurchases one million shares at $40/share, using $40 million of company cash.","A third corporation starts with 100 million shares, repurchases five million and issues three million new shares via valid corporate transactions. The issuer must reconcile gross buybacks to net share changes instead of claiming five percent permanent ownership reduction.","Household available investment cash is distinct from a $12,000 future home down-payment requirement. A hypothetical single-stock position of $5,000 can lose 30%, while a bank deposit or suitably low-risk liquid instrument has different risks. No one can assume the stock's future dividends finance the closing.","An academic company memo must identify actual SEC filing documents that a student would consult for a real issuer: 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, proxy, audit report, statement of equity, accounting policies and notes. Do not invent a real public company or pretend fictional case figures are filed SEC facts.","Students must prepare separate earnings, operating cash flow, enterprise and shareholder equity analyses; reconcile dates and denominators; use source dates and provide assumptions, falsifying evidence and a household suitability decision."],"tasks":["Explain whether buying Alder shares on an exchange supplies issuer cash and identify actual residual ownership and legal voting rights versus corporate property title.","Compute original market capitalization, issuance gross proceeds, new share count and 100,000-share dilution percentage, distinguishing issuer proceeds and secondary trading.","Compute simplified enterprise value and explain why market capitalization, share price and enterprise value are different metrics.","Construct the separate small illustrative income statement with gross, operating and net profit margins, and basic EPS at 30,000 weighted-average shares.","Reconcile indirect operating cash flow from net income and working-capital changes, then subtract capex to compute simplified free cash flow.","Compute current and quick ratios and identify at least three ways accrual earnings or liquid assets might be overstated economically.","Build the separate enterprise FCFF from $100m revenue, margin, tax, D&A, capex and net working capital change; show correct claimant/discount alignment.","Compute WACC from the supplied market-value financing weights and assumptions, and demonstrate sensitivity of year-five terminal value to discount and growth rates.","Bridge $500m enterprise value to common equity, compute per-share value at 10m versus incorrect 8m shares, and compare with fictional $40 quote.","Calculate total stock holding-period return, price-only return, dividend yield where supported, and explain why ex-dividend cash is not a free gain.","Compute earnings and FCF dividend coverage, then stress the cash payout when FCF drops in half.","Analyze EPS after a repurchase and show why buying shares above intrinsic value can reduce remaining shareholders' per-share wealth despite EPS growth.","Reconcile share count after 5m repurchases and 3m issues, and critically evaluate claims of gross buyback yield.","Propose a capital allocation policy comparing reinvestment, debt repayment, dividends and repurchases, then write a primary-source equity research memo with bear/base/bull outcomes and a mortgage cash-reserve constraint."],"answers":["Common shares confer company-equity rights, not direct withdrawal rights over corporate assets; a typical secondary-market buyer pays a selling investor rather than newly financing the issuer.","Original market cap 10m×$40=$400m. New issuance 2m×$20=$40m gross proceeds, shares 12m. Holder originally 100,000/10m=1%, afterward 100,000/12m≈0.83333% (relative ownership decline 16.67%).","Simplified EV = $400m market common equity+$150m debt−$50m excess cash=$500m. Equity cap is not free cash, nor EV is the same as common market value.","Revenue $1m−$600k cost of sales=$400k gross profit (40%); less $250k opex=$150k operating (15%); less $30k interest and $30k taxes=$90k net (9%). Basic EPS in the separately scaled 30k weighted-average share example=$3.","CFO=$90k+$20k−$35k−$15k+$10k=$70k. Simple FCF=CFO $70k−capex $45k=$25k; earnings and cash are distinct.","Current ratio $300k/$200k=1.50; quick ratio ($100k+$120k)/$200k=1.10. Receivable collectability, inventory markdowns, debt maturities and restricted cash may require adjustment.","EBIT $100m×20%=$20m, NOPAT $15m, FCFF=$15m+$4m−$9m−$3m=$7m. Use an enterprise-consistent discount rate for FCFF rather than an equity-only rate.","Illustrative WACC 0.6×10%+0.4×6%×(1−25%)=7.8%. Year-five terminal value for next-year $10m FCFF at 8% discount and 3% growth=$200m; at 9% and 3% growth=$166.67m; at 8% and 4%=$250m. Discount horizon values further back to present.","Enterprise $500m−net debt $150m=$350m equity. With 10m diluted shares=$35/share; wrong 8m count would claim $43.75. Quote $40 exceeds the $35 modeled value by about 14.29% of model value.","Price-only return ($54−$50)/$50=8%; combined cash distribution and price return ($54−$50+$1.50)/$50=11%. Ex-dividend price typically adjusts approximately for distributed cash other things equal; no free created wealth.","Dividends $40m/$200m net income=20% earnings payout; FCF=$120m−$70m=$50m; cash payout ratio=$40m/$50m=80%. Under 50% FCF stress, $25m FCF gives 160% payout and $15m cash coverage gap.","Initial repurchase EPS=$100m/10m=$10; after one million shares bought, unchanged net income yields $100m/9m≈$11.11 EPS, omitting interest and opportunity effects. In separate intrinsic example, $300m equity less $40m repurchase cash=$260m, divided by 9m shares≈$28.89 versus $30 prior.","Shares begin 100m, retire 5m, issue 3m ⇒ 98m ending count and net 2% reduction, not 5%. Actual weighted diluted shares and stock compensation tax/cash effects require filings.","Full recommendation must name price and filing vintage, demonstrate independent statement reconciliation, project cash after reinvestment, consider leverage and dilution, quantify bear/base/bull price ranges, and preserve the separate $12k home closing liquidity. It can rationally decline to invest."],"rubric":["Security ownership, corporate structure, dilution and filing trace (20%)","Three-statement math, cash conversion and quality of earnings (25%)","Enterprise DCF, WACC, debt and per-share value (25%)","Dividends, buybacks and capital allocation economics (20%)","Household suitability, skepticism and sources (10%)"],"sources":[["SEC Investors How To Read 10-K","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/researching-investments/how-read-10-k"],["SEC Financial Statement Guide","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/begfinstmtguide.htm"],["SEC EDGAR Filing Lookup","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"],["Investor.gov Stock Basics","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks"],["IRS Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["FINRA Stock Risks","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/stocks"]]},"rules":{"originalReaderRetained":true,"noLocalStorageWrites":true,"hypotheticalIssuerOnly":true,"notAccredited":true,"statementScaleContextsIndependent":true}};
